TI  - Airway Foreign Bodies.
LID - S0030-6665(26)00030-7 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.otc.2026.03.004 [doi]
AB  - Pediatric airway foreign bodies are common and potentially life-threatening 
      situations. Ingestion of organic material in children under 3 is the most common 
      clinical situation. Typical cases have a history suspicious for a sudden 
      aspiration event supported by physical findings of wheezing or diminished breath 
      sounds with radiological evidence of air-trapping. Rigid bronchoscopy remains the 
      gold standard for diagnosis and treatment and providers should have a low 
      threshold to proceed to the operating room if the clinical suspicion for 
      aspiration is high. Computed tomography (CT) scans may be helpful to further 
      guide decision-making in cases where there is low or intermediate suspicion.
