TI  - Lipoid Pneumonia From Bug Spray: A Case Report.
PG  - e109444
LID - 10.7759/cureus.109444 [doi]
LID - e109444
AB  - Exogenous lipoid pneumonia is an uncommon inflammatory lung disease caused by the 
      aspiration or inhalation of lipid-containing substances. Clinical presentation is 
      highly variable, ranging from asymptomatic disease to progressive respiratory 
      failure, and the condition is often underrecognized in clinical practice. 
      Diagnosis can be challenging because of nonspecific symptoms and imaging 
      findings. We report a case of a 38-year-old woman with suspected exogenous lipoid 
      pneumonia associated with exposure to a commercially available bug spray 
      containing plant-based oils. Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage revealed 
      eosinophilia and a markedly elevated lipid-laden macrophage index, supporting the 
      diagnosis of lipoid pneumonia. The patient was treated with systemic 
      corticosteroids and advised to avoid further exposure, resulting in clinical 
      improvement and near-complete radiographic resolution on follow-up imaging. This 
      case highlights the importance of obtaining a detailed exposure history in 
      patients with persistent respiratory symptoms, as recognition of lipid exposure 
      can facilitate timely diagnosis and targeted management.
