TI  - Primary Mediastinal Osteosarcoma: A Rare and Aggressive Tumour in an Unusual 
      Location.
PG  - 006026
LID - 10.12890/2026_006026 [doi]
LID - 006026
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Mediastinal osteosarcoma is an exceptionally rare and aggressive 
      malignancy arising in a location devoid of osseous structures. Unlike 
      conventional osteosarcoma, which originates in the metaphyseal regions of long 
      bones in younger individuals, mediastinal osteosarcoma develops within soft 
      tissue, leading to a poorly understood pathogenesis and unpredictable clinical 
      behaviour. Owing to its nonspecific symptoms and imaging overlap with other 
      mediastinal tumours, early diagnosis is challenging. To date, around ten 
      well-documented cases have been reported. CASE DESCRIPTION: A 41-year-old man 
      presented with progressive dyspnoea of 3 months' duration. Laboratory tests were 
      unremarkable except for elevated alkaline phosphatase. Computed tomography 
      revealed a 10 × 8.8 cm calcified anterior mediastinal mass with multiple 
      calcified pulmonary nodules and lymphadenopathy. Robotic bronchoscopy of a 
      minimally calcified right middle lobe nodule revealed a high-grade sarcomatoid 
      malignancy forming malignant osteoid. Tumour cells expressed SATB2 (Special 
      AT-rich sequence-binding protein 2) and were negative for epithelial markers 
      (Pancytokeratin, TTF-1, Napsin A, and P40). The findings favoured a diagnosis of 
      extraskeletal osteosarcoma. Positron emission tomography/computed tomography scan 
      demonstrated distant metastases to the brain and the absence of any primary bone 
      lesions. The case was discussed in a multidisciplinary tumour board, and combined 
      chemotherapy with Cisplatin and Doxorubicin was initiated, along with 
      radiotherapy targeting the intracranial lesion, followed by Regorafenib, a kinase 
      inhibitor. CONCLUSION: Mediastinal osteosarcoma should be considered in the 
      differential diagnosis of calcified mediastinal masses. Accurate diagnosis 
      depends on adequate tissue sampling and immunohistochemical profiling. Continued 
      reporting of such rare cases is essential to improve understanding of clinical 
      behaviour and optimize therapeutic strategies for these tumours. LEARNING POINTS: 
      Primary mediastinal osteosarcoma is a rare disease.Calcifications within a 
      mediastinal tumour should raise suspicion of osteosarcoma.Due to the rarity of 
      mediastinal osteosarcoma, there is no consensus on how to treat this tumour.
