TI  - Expectorated bronchial casts revealing lymphatic plastic bronchitis due to 
      thoracic duct agenesis: case report.
LID - 10.1186/s12890-026-04432-7 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Plastic bronchitis is a rare disorder most often linked to aberrant 
      lymphatic drainage or congenital and acquired cardiac anomalies. In patients with 
      lymphatic flow abnormalities, leakage or anomalous lymphatic-bronchial 
      communications can allow lymphatic fluid to enter the airways, where it 
      solidifies into gelatinous or rubbery bronchial casts that may obstruct airflow 
      and are typically expectorated. CASE PRESENTATION: A 49-year-old man presented 
      with chronic, recurrent expectoration of pale bronchial casts accompanied by 
      episodic dyspnea and intermittent fever. A chest CT demonstrated right-lower-lobe 
      ground-glass opacities, interstitial changes with a lymphatic distribution 
      pattern, and mediastinal lymphadenopathy. Bronchoscopy was unremarkable, and 
      sputum microbiology was negative. Pulmonary function testing showed a mild 
      obstruction. Cytologic evaluation of an expelled cast revealed mucus with fibrin, 
      erythrocytes, macrophages, lymphocytes, and neutrophils. MR lymphangiography 
      identified a congenital absence of the thoracic duct. Abdominal lymph drained 
      through two markedly dilated lymphatic vessels converging in the retromediastinum 
      and supplying predominantly right-sided pulmonary lymphatics, with a normal 
      left-sided system. These findings established the diagnosis of lymphatic plastic 
      bronchitis due to thoracic duct agenesis. The patient declined lymphatic 
      intervention aimed at occluding the subdiaphragmatic channels, and his condition 
      remains chronically persistent. CONCLUSION: This case highlights an unusual and 
      unexpected presentation of bronchial cast formation. While bronchial casts are 
      typically identified and removed bronchoscopically-often preserving the 
      morphology of the bronchial tree-in this patient the casts were not amenable to 
      bronchoscopic removal, and were instead repeatedly expectorated as cohesive 
      clots. Spontaneous expectoration of bronchial casts is a rare phenomenon and 
      underscores the variability in clinical presentation and management challenges of 
      this condition.
