TI  - Multidisciplinary Management of Tracheal Perforation: A Case of Successful 
      Reconstruction and Stenting.
PG  - e70661
LID - 10.1002/rcr2.70661 [doi]
LID - e70661
AB  - Airway perforation is a potentially lethal complication from tracheal or 
      mediastinal malignancies that require complex management. Advanced thyroid cancer 
      is known to have high morbidity, and patients with tracheal metastatic lesions 
      are vulnerable to injury. Therapeutic management typically comprises a 
      combination of surgical and nonoperative treatment modalities, whose adverse 
      effects can lead to significant airway injury. We present the case of a 
      67-year-old man with papillary thyroid carcinoma who developed a tracheocutaneous 
      fistula following proton beam therapy and chemotherapy for metastatic disease. 
      Airway repair and recanalization were successfully achieved through a combined 
      surgical and bronchoscopic approach.
