TI  - A case report on αIN antibody-mediated paraneoplastic cerebellar ataxia.
PG  - 109338
LID - S0303-8467(26)00030-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.clineuro.2026.109338 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: α-internexin (αIN) antibody-associated paraneoplastic cerebellar 
      ataxia is a rare neurological disorder, with limited cases reported worldwide. 
      This study aims to present a detailed clinical course and diagnostic approach for 
      this condition. CASE PRESENTATION: A 68-year-old female presented with subacute 
      blurred vision, lower limb weakness, and dysarthria following a SARS-CoV-2 
      infection. Neurological examination revealed nystagmus, dysarthria, and limb 
      ataxia. Arterial spin-labeling MRI showed hypoperfusion in bilateral cerebellar 
      hemispheres. Serum and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) were positive for αIN 
      antibodies. Bronchoscopy suggested small-cell lung cancer. The patient received 
      intravenous immunoglobulin therapy with partial symptom improvement. CONCLUSION: 
      αIN antibodies represent a novel and important biomarker for paraneoplastic 
      neurological syndromes. Early detection and immunotherapy may improve outcomes, 
      particularly when associated with underlying malignancies. This case adds to the 
      limited literature on neuronal intermediate filament autoimmunity.
