TI  - Multisystem IgG4-Related Disease Mimicking Malignancy With Cavitary Pulmonary 
      Nodules, Pleural Thickening and Hepatic Mass-Like Lesions: A Case Report.
PG  - e70669
LID - 10.1002/rcr2.70669 [doi]
LID - e70669
AB  - IgG4-related disease (IgG4-RD) is a multisystem fibroinflammatory condition that 
      can mimic metastatic malignancy. A 49-year-old woman presented with a 6-month 
      history of progressive dyspnoea, nonproductive cough and severe weight loss. 
      Computed tomography demonstrated diffuse bronchocentric and perilymphatic 
      pulmonary nodules with cavitation and consolidation, right pleural thickening and 
      multifocal peripheral-enhancing hepatic mass-like lesions. Given concern for 
      disseminated malignancy, bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage was performed 
      and was negative for infection and malignancy. Lung biopsy showed fibrosis with 
      marked CD138-positive plasma cell infiltration and microabscesses. As concern for 
      malignancy persisted, a liver biopsy was performed for diagnostic clarification. 
      Liver biopsy showed ductular reaction, patchy fibrosis and increased 
      IgG4-positive plasma cells, with elevated serum IgG4 (798.0 mg/dL). The patient 
      met the 2020 revised comprehensive diagnostic criteria for possible IgG4-RD and 
      showed rapid improvement with glucocorticoid therapy. IgG4-RD should be 
      considered in patients with multisystem mass-forming lesions to avoid 
      misdiagnosis and delayed treatment.
