PMID- 42130705
OWN - NLM
STAT- PubMed-not-MEDLINE
DCOM- 20260514
LR  - 20260514
IS  - 2637-3726 (Electronic)
IS  - 2637-3726 (Linking)
VI  - 14
DP  - 2026 Jan-Jun
TI  - Positron emission tomography evidence of tracheal tube-induced posterior 
      laryngeal pressure injury.
PG  - e70064
LID - 10.1002/anr3.70064 [doi]
LID - e70064
AB  - Tracheal tubes are essential for airway management during general anaesthesia but 
      can cause mucosal pressure injuries, especially with prolonged procedures and 
      larger tube sizes. We report a case of a 74-year-old woman who underwent a 
      3.5-hour robot-assisted bronchoscopy with endobronchial biopsies with an 8.0 mm 
      tracheal tube, later presenting with a severe protracted sore throat. Review of 
      her post-procedural positron emission tomography scan uniquely revealed focal 
      F-18 fluorodeoxyglucose uptake along the posterior larynx, consistent with 
      localised inflammation from tracheal intubation, a demonstration which has yet to 
      be described. This case highlights positron emission tomography imaging as an 
      incidental but compelling visualisation of intubation-related laryngeal pressure 
      injury.
