DP  - 2026 Jul 15
TI  - Endobronchial Spindle Cell Tumor of Neurogenic Origin with Coexisting Bronchial 
      Anthracofibrosis: A Rare Case Report.
LID - 10.4103/aam.aam_418_26 [doi]
AB  - Endobronchial spindle cell tumors are rare pulmonary neoplasms that may arise 
      from peripheral nerve sheath elements such as schwannoma or neurofibroma. We 
      report a 69-year-old nonsmoking female from a hilly region presenting with 
      progressive breathlessness for 3 months. She had a significant environmental 
      exposure history of 45 years to biomass fuel combustion from bonfires and 
      fireplace use for cooking and heating. Contrast-enhanced computed tomography (CT) 
      revealed atelectasis with microcalcification in the lateral segment of the right 
      middle lobe and extensive mediastinal lymphadenopathy. F18-fluorodeoxyglucose 
      (FDG) positron emission tomography-CT showed FDG-avid soft tissue in the right 
      middle lobe bronchus (maximum standardized uptake value: 9.17) with multiple 
      FDG-avid lymph nodes. Bronchoscopy demonstrated narrowing of the right middle 
      lobe bronchus with endobronchial obstruction. Histopathology and 
      immunohistochemistry confirmed a low-grade spindle cell tumor of neurogenic 
      origin (neurofibroma/schwannoma) with coexisting anthracofibrosis. The patient 
      was managed using a multidisciplinary approach after comprehensive staging. This 
      case highlights a rare presentation of an endobronchial spindle cell tumor in a 
      nonsmoking female with associated bronchial anthracofibrosis. The unusual 
      endobronchial location and neurogenic origin distinguish it from typical spindle 
      cell tumors. It underscores the importance of considering rare tumors in patients 
      with airway obstruction, the value of detailed exposure history, and the crucial 
      role of bronchoscopic and histopathological evaluation in diagnosis. Early and 
      accurate diagnosis enables appropriate management and is associated with a 
      favorable prognosis.
