TI  - Mid-term efficacy and safety of transbronchial cryoablation for stage IA 
      peripheral lung cancer: a retrospective analysis.
PG  - 79
LID - 10.21037/tlcr-2026-1-0043 [doi]
LID - 79
AB  - BACKGROUND: Transbronchial cryoablation is an emerging treatment option for 
      patients with early-stage lung cancer who are not candidates for surgery or who 
      decline surgery. However, evidence regarding its efficacy and safety remains 
      limited. This study aims to retrospectively evaluate the mid-term efficacy and 
      safety of transbronchial cryoablation in patients with stage IA peripheral lung 
      cancer. METHODS: This single-center retrospective study included patients with 
      stage IA peripheral lung cancer who received transbronchial cryoablation due to 
      unsuitability for or refusal to surgery and radiotherapy from September 2022 to 
      October 2025. All patients underwent transbronchial cryoablation under the 
      guidance of either navigation bronchoscopy or robotic bronchoscopy in combination 
      with cone-beam computed tomography (CBCT). The efficacy was assessed by the local 
      progression-free survival (LPFS), and safety was evaluated based on the incidence 
      of adverse events occurring during or within 30 days after the ablation 
      procedure. RESULTS: A total of 23 patients with 24 tumors underwent 25 
      transbronchial cryoablation procedures. The median age of patients was 67.0 
      years, and the mean lesion size was 14.2±4.2 mm. Among all lesions, six lesions 
      were solid nodules, 18 lesions were ground-glass nodules. The LPFS rate remained 
      stable at 89.2% throughout the follow-up period at 1, 2, and 3 years. Two 
      patients with solid nodules experienced local progression at 6 and 9 months after 
      cryoablation, respectively. Adverse events and intervention-acquired adverse 
      events were reported in 16.0% and 0.0% of participants. CONCLUSIONS: Our findings 
      suggest that transbronchial cryoablation may be an effective and safe option for 
      stage IA peripheral lung cancer; however, definitive confirmation of its efficacy 
      awaits prospective, large-scale randomized controlled trials.
