IS  - 1471-2253 (Linking)
DP  - 2026 Jun 4
TI  - Ultrasound-guided modified superior laryngeal nerve block for awake tracheal 
      intubation in patients with difficult airways.
LID - 10.1186/s12871-026-03972-z [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To evaluate the efficacy of an ultrasound-guided modified superior 
      laryngeal nerve block for awake tracheal intubation in patients with anticipated 
      difficult airways. METHODS: This study included 60 patients with difficult 
      airways who were scheduled for orthopedic surgery under general anesthesia. The 
      study was conducted at the Sixth Affiliated Hospital of Xinjiang Medical 
      University from January 2024 to December 2025. Patients were grouped according to 
      routine clinical practice. The experimental group received an ultrasound-guided 
      modified superior laryngeal nerve block (n = 30), whereas the control group 
      received topical anesthesia via fiberoptic bronchoscope-guided supraglottic 
      spraying (n = 30). Intubation time and success rate were recorded. Cough response 
      was graded, patient satisfaction was assessed, and peri-procedural complications 
      were documented. RESULTS: Baseline characteristics were comparable between the 
      two groups (p > 0.05). Intubation time was shorter in the experimental group than 
      in the control group (1.73 ± 0.44 vs. 2.70 ± 0.46 min; p < 0.001). The success 
      rate was also higher in the experimental group (100% vs. 83%; p = 0.020). Cough 
      responses were attenuated, with 90% of patients in the experimental group showing 
      Grade I responses, whereas Grade III-IV responses were more frequent in the 
      control group (37%) (p = 0.026). Patient comfort was better in the experimental 
      group, with 90% versus 40% rated as "good" (p < 0.001), and poor comfort occurred 
      only in the control group (37%). The incidences of tachycardia and hypertension 
      were lower in the experimental group, although the differences were not 
      statistically significant (p > 0.05). No laryngospasm, bleeding, or nerve injury 
      was observed in either group. CONCLUSION: Ultrasound-guided modified superior 
      laryngeal nerve block was associated with shorter intubation time and attenuated 
      cough response during awake tracheal intubation in patients with difficult 
      airways. Higher procedural success and patient satisfaction were achieved without 
      an increase in complications. This approach appears to be safe and feasible in 
      the described clinical setting.
