DP  - 2026 Jun 30
TI  - Comparison of learning curves between electromagnetic navigation bronchoscopy and 
      Ion robotic bronchoscopy for preoperative localization.
PG  - 584
LID - 10.21037/jtd-2026-0777 [doi]
LID - 584
AB  - BACKGROUND: Electromagnetic navigation bronchoscopy (ENB) and Ion robotic 
      bronchoscopy are novel, high-precision techniques with low complication rates for 
      the preoperative localization of peripheral pulmonary nodules (PPNs). Their 
      clinical application is largely influenced by the operator's learning curve; 
      however, direct, systematic comparisons of the learning curves between these two 
      techniques are scarce. This study therefore aimed to analyze differences in the 
      learning curves between ENB and Ion robotic bronchoscopy for PPN localization. 
      METHODS: A retrospective analysis was performed on the perioperative data of 120 
      patients who underwent preoperative PPN localization (60 consecutive ENB-guided 
      cases and 60 Ion robotic bronchoscopy-guided cases) from September 2024 to June 
      2025. All procedures were performed by the same thoracic surgeon. The clinical 
      characteristics of the two groups were compared, and the cumulative sum (CUSUM) 
      method was employed to analyze operative time (OT). RESULTS: Perioperative 
      comparisons revealed that the ENB group had a significantly shorter OT (17.13 vs. 
      45.62 min in the Ion group), whereas the Ion group exhibited a higher 
      localization success rate (96.67% vs. 86.67% in the ENB group). CUSUM modeling of 
      OT divided the learning curves into learning and proficiency phases, with 
      inflection points at the 19(th) procedure for ENB and the 34(th) for Ion. Further 
      analysis confirmed that the OT during the learning phase was significantly longer 
      than that in the proficiency phase for both groups (P<0.05). CONCLUSIONS: This 
      study demonstrates that ENB-guided PPN localization has a shorter learning curve 
      than Ion robotic bronchoscopy, while the latter achieves a higher localization 
      success rate.
