TI  - EBUS-Guided Incidental Finding of Pulmonary Vein Thrombosis: A Case Report and 
      Literature Review.
PG  - e70612
LID - 10.1002/rcr2.70612 [doi]
LID - e70612
AB  - Pulmonary vein thrombosis (PVT) is an uncommon but potentially fatal condition 
      that is often underdiagnosed due to nonspecific clinical presentation. We report 
      a case of a 47-year-old woman with a thrombus in the right inferior pulmonary 
      vein incidentally found during a robotic-assisted bronchoscopy biopsy (RAB) and 
      endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) performed for mediastinal evaluation and lymph 
      node sampling. PVT was confirmed with computed tomography pulmonary angiography, 
      and the patient was successfully treated with anticoagulation therapy. A review 
      of the literature revealed that reports of PVT detected via EBUS are exceedingly 
      rare and usually malignant in nature. To our knowledge, this is the first 
      peer-reviewed case report describing PVT identified incidentally during EBUS, 
      underscoring the potential of this technique to reveal clinically significant 
      vascular abnormalities.
