TI  - Pulmonary Tuberculosis Masking Lung Adenocarcinoma: A Diagnostic Pitfall 
      Following Apparent Treatment Response.
PG  - e108407
LID - 10.7759/cureus.108407 [doi]
LID - e108407
AB  - Pulmonary tuberculosis and lung cancer may present with overlapping clinical and 
      radiological features, making diagnosis challenging. We report the case of a 
      42-year-old chronic smoker with bacteriologically confirmed pulmonary 
      tuberculosis who showed marked clinical and radiological improvement after 
      anti-tuberculosis therapy. Within 20 days of this apparent favorable evolution, 
      the patient developed hemoptysis associated with rapid clinical deterioration. 
      Imaging revealed a right hilar mass with metastatic disease. Bronchoscopic 
      findings and histopathological examination confirmed lung adenocarcinoma (TTF-1 
      positive). This case highlights how apparent treatment response can be misleading 
      and may delay the recognition of an underlying malignancy. Reassessment should be 
      considered when the clinical course becomes atypical or rapidly progressive, 
      particularly in high-risk patients.
