TI  - A Robotic Left Lower Lobe Sleeve Lobectomy With Bronchoplasty Using the 
      Fissureless Technique After Neoadjuvant Immunochemotherapy.
PG  - 467-470
LID - 10.1016/j.atssr.2025.12.023 [doi]
AB  - Robotic bronchoplastic procedures offer technical advantages over thoracoscopy, 
      particularly in complex situations involving dense fissures or posttreatment 
      fibrosis. We describe a 60-year-old man with squamous cell carcinoma who 
      underwent robotic left lower lobe sleeve lobectomy with bronchoplasty by the 
      fissureless technique after neoadjuvant immunochemotherapy. Preoperative imaging 
      showed tumor regression and an incomplete fissure, with bronchoscopy confirming 
      left main bronchial invasion. The robotic platform enabled precise dissection and 
      anastomosis with continuous barbed suturing. The postoperative course was 
      uneventful, and pathologic examination revealed ypT1c N2b M0 disease. This case 
      demonstrates the feasibility of integrating the fissureless technique into 
      complex robotic bronchoplastic surgery.
