TI  - Enhancing lung health in children with structural damage and malformations: trial 
      protocol for a randomised controlled trial of azithromycin for airway infection 
      prevention.
LID - 01538-2025 [pii]
LID - 10.1183/23120541.01538-2025 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Children with early-onset structural lung damage or lung and airway 
      malformations - congenital or acquired - are highly susceptible to recurrent 
      respiratory tract infections, leading to frequent hospitalisations, impaired lung 
      function and reduced quality of life. Although azithromycin maintenance therapy 
      is commonly used in this population, robust clinical evidence supporting its 
      efficacy is lacking. OBJECTIVES: The primary aim of this trial is to evaluate the 
      efficacy of azithromycin maintenance therapy in reducing respiratory infections 
      among young children with structural lung damage or lung and airway 
      malformations. Secondary objectives are to assess the efficacy of azithromycin on 
      lung function, daily respiratory symptoms and quality of life, as well as the 
      safety of long-term azithromycin use in this age group. METHODS: This is a 
      national, multicentre, double-blind, placebo-controlled randomised clinical trial 
      conducted across three specialised paediatric pulmonology centres in Denmark. A 
      total of 150 children aged 0-72 months with structural lung damage or lung or 
      airway malformations confirmed by bronchoscopy or radiology will be enrolled. 
      Participants will be randomised 1:1 to receive either oral azithromycin 
      (10 mg·kg(-1)) or placebo three times weekly for 6 months during the winter 
      season. ETHICS AND DISSEMINATION: The trial has been approved by the European 
      Medical Agency (EMA). It is expected to provide high-quality evidence on efficacy 
      and safety of azithromycin maintenance therapy in a vulnerable paediatric 
      population with rare respiratory conditions. Positive findings will inform 
      clinical guidelines and help enhance lung health; conversely, if treatment proves 
      ineffective, results may support efforts to reduce unnecessary antibiotic use.
