TI  - A Marked Improvement in Mild but Progressive Autoimmune Pulmonary Alveolar 
      Proteinosis Treated with Inhaled Granulocyte-Macrophage Colony-Stimulating Factor 
      Therapy: A Case Report.
LID - 10.2169/internalmedicine.7389-26 [doi]
AB  - A 46-year-old man was diagnosed with autoimmune pulmonary alveolar proteinosis 
      (aPAP). Despite repeated segmental bronchoscopic lavage, his symptoms, pulmonary 
      function, and radiological findings gradually worsened, and he declined 
      whole-lung lavage. Although classified as disease severity score 2 (partial 
      pressure of arterial oxygen 75.2 mmHg), inhaled granulocyte-macrophage 
      colony-stimulating factor (GM-CSF) therapy (sargramostim) was initiated. After 6 
      months, a marked improvement was observed, and the alveolar-arterial oxygen 
      difference decreased from 21.8 to 1.1 mmHg. Given the limited evidence in milder 
      cases, this report suggests that early intervention with inhaled sargramostim may 
      be effective in relatively mild but progressive aPAP.
