TI  - Acute pulmonary histoplasmosis in a Belgian traveller returning from Suriname 
      diagnosed with NGS: a case report.
PG  - 117461
LID - S0732-8893(26)00211-7 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.diagmicrobio.2026.117461 [doi]
AB  - We describe the case of a 48-year-old Belgian woman who developed fever, dry 
      cough and dyspnoea shortly after returning from a two-week trip to Suriname. CT 
      scan of the thorax revealed 2 nodular masses with surrounding ground glass 
      alterations in the right lung as well as multiple hilar and mediastinal 
      lymphadenopathies. Bronchoscopy with broncho-alveolar lavage and endobronchial 
      ultrasound-guided transbronchial needle aspiration of thoracic lymph nodes were 
      performed. Broncho-alveolar lavage fluid showed no abnormalities, including a 
      negative PCR for Aspergillus spp., and negative cryptococcal and galactomannan 
      antigen tests. (1→3)-β-D-glucan determination on serum was also negative. 
      Pathological analysis of all punctions showed granulomatous lymphadenitis and the 
      presence of small, rounded structures on Grocott's staining. Fungal 
      identification by targeted nanopore sequencing on lymph node tissue resulted in a 
      weak but specific signal for Histoplasma capsulatum. Our case illustrates the 
      diagnostic value of next-generation sequencing for a rare fungal disease.
