TI  - Progressive unilobar cavitating lung disease despite medical therapy: Diagnostic 
      limitations and definitive surgical management.
PG  - 102432
LID - 10.1016/j.rmcr.2026.102432 [doi]
LID - 102432
AB  - Cavitating lung lesions are a common radiological finding with a broad 
      differential diagnosis, including infectious, malignant and inflammatory causes. 
      Management of infective lesion is often challenging, particularly when disease 
      progresses despite appropriate medical therapy. We report a case of progressive, 
      unilobar cavitating lung disease in a 59-year-old man related to Aspergillus 
      colonization and Streptococcus constellatus infection, despite prolonged optimal 
      antibacterial and antifungal treatment. Initial bronchoscopic investigations 
      identified Aspergillus hyphae, but repeated conventional microbiological sampling 
      failed to detect the underlying bacterial pathogen. Serial imaging demonstrated 
      persistent progressive cavitation coalescing into large fluid-filled abscess 
      confined to upper lobe, prompting surgical intervention. The bacterial pathogen 
      was only identified after lobectomy, with cultures confirming S. constellatus. 
      This case highlights the limitations of standard diagnostic techniques, the 
      importance of recognising failure of medical therapy and the role of timely 
      thoracic surgical resection in achieving definitive source control in 
      anatomically localised infective cavitating lung disease.
