TI  - Endovascular Management of Interlobolar Bronchopulmonary Arterial Fistula After 
      Bronchial Stent Removal.
PG  - 109212
LID - S2666-0849(26)02373-9 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jaccas.2026.109212 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Bronchopulmonary arterial fistulas are rare, life-threatening causes 
      of hemoptysis, often occurring in patients with thoracic malignancy, radiation 
      exposure, or repeated airway instrumentation. CASE SUMMARY: A 36-year-old woman 
      with metastatic epithelioid sarcoma and radiation-induced bronchus intermedius 
      stenosis underwent multiple bronchoscopic dilations and airway stent placements. 
      Following bronchial stent removal, she developed massive airway hemorrhage. 
      Computed tomography angiography demonstrated bronchopulmonary arterial fistula. 
      Given prohibitive surgical risk, endovascular exclusion with a covered pulmonary 
      arterial stent achieved immediate hemostasis. Recurrent hemoptysis due to 
      incomplete stent apposition was successfully treated with repeat angioplasty. The 
      patient remained clinically stable on follow-up. DISCUSSION: Successful 
      endovascular management of bronchovascular fistulas offers effective hemorrhage 
      control while preserving pulmonary perfusion in nonsurgical candidates. TAKE-HOME 
      MESSAGES: Endovascular therapy can serve as a salvage option for bronchopulmonary 
      arterial fistulas in high-risk patients. Multidisciplinary coordination, 
      postprocedural surveillance, and lifelong prophylaxis are essential for durable 
      outcomes.
