LID - 102115
AB  - BACKGROUND: Immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI)-related pneumonitis is a 
      well-recognized immune-related adverse event; however, its manifestation as 
      organizing pneumonia (OP) mimicking metastatic disease remains underrecognized 
      and presents a significant diagnostic challenge. Distinguishing true tumor 
      progression from inflammatory processes is critical to avoid inappropriate 
      treatment decisions. CASE PRESENTATION: A 60-year-old woman with high-grade 
      serous endometrial carcinoma (pT2N2mi, pMMR) treated with adjuvant carboplatin, 
      paclitaxel, and dostarlimab followed by maintenance dostarlimab. Nineteen months 
      after diagnosis, routine surveillance CT and FDG-PET/CT demonstrated new 
      bilateral FDG-avid pleural-based masses and pulmonary consolidations (SUV up to 
      8.9), raising strong concern for metastatic progression. Despite these findings, 
      the patient remained asymptomatic, and serial circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) 
      testing was persistently negative. Initial bronchoscopy was non-diagnostic. A 
      subsequent CT-guided core needle biopsy of a pleural lesion revealed organizing 
      pneumonia with non-necrotizing granulomatous inflammation and no evidence of 
      malignancy, establishing a diagnosis of ICI-related OP. Immunotherapy was 
      discontinued, and corticosteroid therapy was initiated, resulting in complete 
      radiographic resolution within two months. CONCLUSION: ICI-related OP may present 
      with mass-like, FDG-avid lesions that closely resemble metastatic disease. This 
      case underscores the essential role of tissue biopsy in confirming the diagnosis 
      and preventing misclassification of disease progression. Additionally, 
      persistently negative ctDNA may serve as a supportive, noninvasive adjunct in 
      distinguishing inflammatory processes from true malignancy.
