TI  - Removal of a Fishhook-Shaped Foreign Body in the Bronchus and Literature Review.
PG  - e70641
LID - 10.1002/rcr2.70641 [doi]
LID - e70641
AB  - Foreign body aspiration, particularly involving complex metallic objects with 
      sharp projections like fishhooks, presents a formidable therapeutic challenge, 
      often leading to complications such as mucosal impaction and obstructive 
      pneumonia. We report a challenging case of a metallic fishhook-shaped foreign 
      body deeply embedded within the distal bronchial wall, where initial attempts at 
      retrieval using standard biopsy and foreign body forceps via flexible 
      bronchoscopy were unsuccessful due to significant mucosal oedema and impaction. 
      The successful management involved a novel, multi-modal endoscopic strategy that 
      sequentially integrated the stability of a rigid bronchoscope, the precise 
      navigation of an ultrathin bronchoscope for direct visualisation of the embedded 
      tip and the controlled dissection using a high-frequency electrocautery knife to 
      safely release the object, followed by its retrieval. This case demonstrates a 
      reproducible, stepwise surgical strategy for the safe extraction of similarly 
      complex, impacted sharp foreign bodies, thereby minimising the risk of iatrogenic 
      injury.
