TI  - A lung squamous cell carcinoma demonstrating spontaneous partial regression of 
      the primary tumor with lymph node metastasis: a case report with analysis of the 
      tumor immune microenvironment.
PG  - 109
LID - 10.21037/tlcr-2025-1-1466 [doi]
LID - 109
AB  - BACKGROUND: Spontaneous regression (SR) of malignant tumors is a well-known but 
      rare phenomenon in lung cancer. Previous studies have suggested that antitumor 
      immune responses may contribute to SR. CASE DESCRIPTION: We report the case of a 
      74-year-old man with a right lower lobe pulmonary nodule that demonstrated 
      regression during observation, accompanied by mediastinal lymph node (LN) 
      metastasis after nondiagnostic bronchoscopic and needle biopsies. Surgical 
      resection confirmed squamous cell carcinoma with subcarinal LN metastasis. The 
      primary tumor was largely replaced by fibrosis, with residual carcinoma cells 
      comprising only 30% of the lesion. Immunohistochemistry (IHC) demonstrated marked 
      infiltration of multiple T-cell subsets in the primary tumor, whereas the 
      metastatic lesion exhibited reduced immune infiltration. 
      Antigen-presentation-related markers, including β2-microglobulin (B2M), human 
      leukocyte antigen-DR (HLA-DR), and programmed death-ligand 1 (PD-L1), were 
      expressed in the primary lesion but were lost in the metastasis. Overall, the 
      primary lesion showed dense infiltration of CD8(+) and CD103(+) T cells, whereas 
      the metastatic LN exhibited reduced immune cell infiltration together with loss 
      of antigen-presentation-related marker expression. CONCLUSIONS: By comparing the 
      immune microenvironments in the primary tumor and the metastatic LN, this case 
      suggests that differences in local immune context may underlie the distinct 
      clinical behavior of the two lesions.
