TI  - An Unusual Cause of Extubation Failure: Surgical Fixation of the Endotracheal 
      Tube With a Suture-A Case Report.
PG  - e02254
LID - 10.1213/XAA.0000000000002254 [doi]
AB  - Technical difficulties encountered during endotracheal tube (ETT) extubation are 
      rare but can precipitate catastrophic airway complications. We present the case 
      of a 3-month-old male infant weighing 5.4 kg who underwent video-assisted 
      thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) for a bronchogenic cyst excision. Following the 
      procedure, initial attempts at extubation failed. Flexible fiberoptic 
      bronchoscopy (FOB) identified that the ETT had been inadvertently sutured to the 
      tracheal wall. Since this Vicryl suture also secured the surgical repair, 
      extubation was delayed to avoid disrupting the anastomosis. On postoperative day 
      three, the suture was safely transected from within the trachea using 
      laser-assisted FOB. This case suggests that a post-procedural FOB examination 
      could be a valuable tool for evaluating patency and anastomotic stability in 
      selected high-risk airway reconstructions. It also highlights the importance of 
      interdisciplinary communication and vigilance between surgical and anesthesia 
      teams during complex thoracic procedures.
