TI  - Bronchiolitis Obliterans as a Delayed Sequela of Mycoplasma pneumoniae 
      Complicated by Stevens-Johnson Syndrome in a Pediatric Patient.
PG  - e71610
LID - 10.1002/ppul.71610 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To report a rare and severe case of bronchiolitis obliterans (BO) 
      developing as a late complication following Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection and 
      associated Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) in a pediatric patient. CASE 
      PRESENTATION: We describe a previously healthy 6-year-old girl who was 
      hospitalized for Mycoplasma pneumoniae complicated by SJS. After resolution of 
      the acute mucocutaneous manifestations, she developed persistent cough, wheezing, 
      and exercise intolerance. Bronchoscopy revealed stenosis and occlusion of 
      multiple segmental and subsegmental bronchi due to granulation tissue, leading to 
      a diagnosis of BO. CONCLUSION: This case underscores that BO can occur as a 
      sequel to Mycoplasma pneumoniae-associated SJS. It highlights the importance of 
      long-term respiratory surveillance and a multidisciplinary approach in the 
      management of such patients.
