TI  - Fibrosing Mediastinitis in Pregnancy: A Rare Case With Antepartum Hemoptysis and 
      Airway Obstruction.
PG  - 106496
LID - S2666-0849(25)03283-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jaccas.2025.106496 [doi]
LID - 106496
AB  - BACKGROUND: Fibrosing mediastinitis (FM) is a rare, progressive disorder 
      characterized by dense fibrotic encasement of mediastinal structures, leading to 
      airway and vascular compromise. Pregnancy-associated FM is exceptionally 
      uncommon, with fewer than 2 dozen cases reported. CASE SUMMARY: We present a case 
      of a 37-year-old woman, gravida 1 para 0, with known FM who developed antepartum 
      hemoptysis and left lung atelectasis at 23 weeks of pregnancy. Computed 
      tomography scan of the chest showed an 80% narrowing of the left main bronchus 
      due to a calcified mediastinal mass. She was managed conservatively with 
      nebulizers and physiotherapy, followed by bronchoscopy with balloon dilation of 
      the left main bronchus. Delivery via classical cesarean at 24 weeks resulted in a 
      viable neonate. DISCUSSION: Physiologic changes during pregnancy can exacerbate 
      cardiopulmonary compromise in FM, requiring multidisciplinary co-ordination. 
      TAKE-HOME MESSAGES: FM may remain stable during pregnancy, and conservative 
      management with close pulmonary and obstetric collaboration can achieve favorable 
      outcomes. Further evaluation with a computed tomography chest scan may be 
      required to assess the need for intervention when oxygenation and hemodynamic 
      status are compromised.
