PB  - StatPearls Publishing
DP  - 2026 Jan
TI  - Mediastinal Trauma.
BTI - StatPearls
AB  - Mediastinal trauma refers to an injury involving the central compartment of the 
      thoracic cavity, which houses several critical anatomical structures. The 
      mediastinum is often categorized into 3 clinically relevant sections: anterior, 
      middle, and posterior. The anterior mediastinum contains the thymus and the 
      internal thoracic branches, surrounded by connective and adipose tissue that 
      cushions the vital cardiac structures underlying it in the middle mediastinum. 
      The middle contains the pericardium, heart, ascending aorta, and great vessels, 
      and trachea/main bronchi. The posterior mediastinum contains the esophagus, 
      descending aorta, intrathoracic vena cava, thoracic duct, and azygos/hemiazygos 
      veins. In trauma surgery, particular attention to mediastinal injury should be 
      paid for penetrating injuries to the “cardiac box,” whose anatomical borders are 
      the midclavicular lines laterally, the sternal notch superiorly, and the tip of 
      the xiphoid inferiorly. Given the importance of these structures and their 
      proximity, traumatic injury to this area can be immediately life-threatening. A 
      large portion of individuals with trauma who experience mediastinal injuries may 
      not make it to the hospital alive, and survival is contingent upon minimizing the 
      time from injury to treatment. Historically, mandatory operative exploration was 
      the primary practice. However, all patients should be treated according to the 
      principles of Advanced Trauma Life Support (ATLS). With advancements in modern 
      diagnostic and radiologic modalities (such as combined arteriography, 
      esophagoscopy, bronchoscopy, and echocardiography), unnecessary explorations can 
      now be avoided.
