TI  - Comparison of Ease of Awake Intubation Using Traditional Fibreoptic Bronchoscope 
      Versus Hybrid Technique with Videolaryngoscopic Assistance in Patients with 
      Difficult Airway.
LID - 10.4103/aam.aam_408_25 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND AND AIMS: Difficult airways pose a significant challenge for 
      intubation. While video laryngoscopes and fiberoptic bronchoscopes (FOB) are 
      effective, they have limitations when used alone. A hybrid technique combining 
      both may improve success. Our study's primary objective was to compare intubation 
      time between a traditional fiberoptic method versus a hybrid videolaryngoscopic 
      technique in patients with difficult airways. Secondary objectives included 
      assessing ease of intubation, number of attempts, and patient comfort. METHODS: 
      This prospective, randomized, observational study enrolled 60 patients with a 
      difficult airway. The airway of patients was topicalized, and those having a 
      percentage of glottic opening score 0 with C-MAC videoscope during awake check 
      laryngoscopy were included in the study. Patients were randomized into two 
      groups: Group F was intubated by traditional awake fiberoptic intubation (AFOI). 
      Awake intubation in Group H was performed using a hybrid technique (FOB with 
      C-MAC videolaryngoscopic assistance). Time to intubate, ease of intubation, 
      number of attempts, and patient comfort score were noted. RESULTS: Mean time to 
      intubation was significantly shorter in Group H (35.57 ± 13.984 s) compared to 
      Group F (76.80 ± 26.484 s, P < 0.001). Intubation was "Easy" in 86.7% of Group H 
      patients versus 33.33% in Group F (P < 0.001). The number of intubation attempts 
      and hemodynamic stability were comparable between groups. However, patient 
      comfort was significantly higher in Group F, with 70% reporting no reaction 
      compared to 23.33% in Group H (P < 0.001). CONCLUSION: The hybrid technique of 
      AFOI with videolaryngoscopic assistance significantly reduced time and improved 
      ease of intubation, though with reduced patient comfort.
