TI  - Precision management of community-acquired pneumonia in children: An applied 
      study combining traditional detection of alveolar lavage fluid pathogenesis with 
      multiplexed targeted nanopore sequencing.
PG  - e44806
LID - 10.1097/MD.0000000000044806 [doi]
AB  - This study aimed to evaluate the efficacy of traditional alveolar lavage fluid 
      pathogenicity detection methods and multiplexed targeted nanopore sequencing 
      (NTS) in identifying pathogens in children with community-acquired pneumonia. 
      Additionally, we analyzed serological characteristics by pathogen type to inform 
      future precision treatment strategies. We retrospectively analyzed data from 251 
      children (aged < 14 years) diagnosed with pneumonia between June 2024 and January 
      2025 at the Department of Pediatric Internal Medicine, Shaoxing People's 
      Hospital. Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid samples were collected via fiberoptic 
      bronchoscopy. Pathogen detection was performed using routine bacterial culture, 
      polymerase chain reaction, and NTS. Serological tests, including white blood cell 
      count, C-reactive protein (CRP), procalcitonin (PCT), D-dimer, lactate 
      dehydrogenase (LDH), and erythrocyte sedimentation rate, were also conducted, and 
      the results were analyzed statistically. Pathogens were detected in 94.42% of the 
      children with pneumonia (237/251). Mycoplasma infections were found in 72.38% of 
      children over 6 years of age, while bacterial infections were more prevalent in 
      children under 6 years. Among 53 cases tested concurrently, the bacterial 
      detection rate of NTS was 37.74%, significantly higher than that of conventional 
      culture (18.87%, P < .05). Significant differences in serological markers were 
      observed between children infected with different pathogens. Specifically, 
      leukocyte count and PCT were lower in the mycoplasma-infected group than in the 
      non-mycoplasma-infected group, while D-dimer and LDH levels were higher 
      (P < .05). This study demonstrated that NTS significantly improved the pathogen 
      detection rate in children with community-acquired pneumonia, particularly in 
      cases of complicated infections, compared to traditional detection methods. 
      Additionally, significant differences in serological markers among children 
      infected with different pathogens were identified, providing valuable insights 
      for clinical diagnosis and treatment. The integration of traditional diagnostic 
      methods with NTS technology may improve diagnostic accuracy, though its impact on 
      clinical management requires further validation.
