TI  - First report of the real-time observation of mucociliary clearance using 
      high-definition bronchoscopy.
PG  - 1030-1032
LID - S2212-5345(25)00123-6 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.resinv.2025.07.022 [doi]
AB  - The airways are naturally equipped with mucociliary escalators, a primary defense 
      mechanism for removing foreign substances. Mucociliary clearance velocity has 
      traditionally been assessed using radiolabeled aerosols; however, real-time 
      dynamic observations have yet to be described. A high-definition bronchoscope was 
      used to observe foamy secretions on the tracheal mucus layer. These secretions 
      moved slowly in a counterclockwise direction at 2.26 cm/min, as measured using 
      distance analysis software. In contrast, movement was noticeably slower in a 
      young adult with recurrent pneumonia. This real-time observation has the 
      potential to offer a minimally invasive method for quantifying mucociliary 
      clearance by tracking the movement of mucus on the epithelial surface. These 
      findings may contribute to understanding individual and pathological variations 
      in airway epithelial mucus dynamics.
