DP  - 2025 Apr
TI  - Post-RSV Bronchial Web Formation in a Lung Transplant Recipient: A Case Report.
PG  - e70195
LID - 10.1002/rcr2.70195 [doi]
LID - e70195
AB  - Bronchial webs (BW) are fibrinous membranes which obstruct the airway lumen. A 
      33-year-old female presented with acute lung allograft dysfunction following a 
      recent respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) infection, having undergone bilateral 
      lung transplant 2 years prior. Bronchoscopy identified subsegmental pale, 
      translucent webs occluding six subsegments across both lung allografts. Each 
      subsegment was dilated with an 8 mm balloon dilatation catheter. There was 
      immediate symptomatic improvement without significant spirometric improvement. 
      The described association between CLAD and BW may reflect an unusual airway 
      complication of the bronchiolitis obliterans (BOS) CLAD phenotype, and the 
      association with RSV infection suggests it has a possible role in the 
      pathogenesis. This is in keeping with the paucity of data on this topic, where BW 
      have been associated with BOS in patients with lung transplants and 
      haematopoietic stem cell transplants. This is the first description of BW 
      formation following RSV infection in a patient with CLAD, suggesting a 
      pathophysiologic role of viral infection.
