TI  - Perioperative Management for Patients With Spinal Muscular Atrophy Undergoing 
      Scoliosis Surgery: A Single-Center Retrospective Study.
PG  - 1328-1341
LID - 10.1111/os.70341 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) is a rare neuromuscular disorder 
      frequently associated with progressive scoliosis, often requiring complex 
      surgical intervention. Perioperative management for SMA patients remains 
      challenging due to severe respiratory compromise, difficult airway anatomy, and a 
      high risk of perioperative complications. This study aimed to analyze 
      perioperative strategies and outcomes in SMA patients undergoing posterior spinal 
      fusion for scoliosis correction. METHODS: We retrospectively reviewed SMA 
      patients who underwent posterior spinal fusion at our institution from January 
      2018 to December 2024. Primary outcomes included intraoperative blood loss, 
      postoperative complications, and perioperative hypokalemia; secondary outcomes 
      included postoperative mechanical ventilation duration, length of stay in the ICU 
      and hospital, and radiographic outcomes. Subgroup comparisons between SMA type 2 
      and type 3 were performed using the independent-samples t-test or Mann-Whitney U 
      test for continuous variables and the chi-square test or Fisher's exact test for 
      categorical variables. RESULTS: Forty-three SMA patients (72.1% type 2, 27.9% 
      type 3) with a mean age of 15.9 ± 4.9 years were included. Spinal deformity was 
      severe, with a mean preoperative Cobb angle of 113.2° ± 22.8°. Pulmonary function 
      was markedly impaired, with a median forced vital capacity (FVC) of 34.8% (IQR: 
      24.6%-46.4%) of the predicted value. Endotracheal intubation was challenging, 
      with 93.0% (n = 40) requiring video laryngoscopy or awake tracheal intubation 
      using flexible bronchoscopy. Blood loss was substantial, with a median 
      intraoperative blood loss of 1200 mL (IQR: 1000-1550 mL) and a postoperative 
      drainage of 1040 mL (IQR: 723-1293 mL). An extraordinarily high incidence of 
      perioperative hypokalemia was observed, affecting 65.1% of patients (n = 28) 
      intraoperatively and 79.1% (n = 34) postoperatively. All patients successfully 
      underwent surgery with a mean Cobb angle correction rate of 49.0% ± 13.5%. There 
      were no cases of operative mortality, and the overall complication rate was low 
      (2.3%, n = 1). SMA type 2 and type 3 shared similar perioperative parameters. 
      CONCLUSIONS: This study represents the largest case series to date in Asia on the 
      perioperative management of SMA patients undergoing scoliosis surgery. These 
      patients present complex perioperative risks requiring meticulous 
      multidisciplinary care. Advanced airway planning, hemodynamic support, blood 
      management strategies, and proactive correction of electrolyte imbalances may 
      help support safer perioperative care. With appropriate planning, scoliosis 
      surgery can be safely performed in SMA patients, offering structural and 
      functional benefits.
