DP  - 2026 Apr 2
TI  - Real-world diagnostic yield and safety of electromagnetic navigation bronchoscopy 
      for pulmonary nodules: Experience of a single centre.
LID - 10.1055/a-2831-4870 [doi]
AB  - Electromagnetic navigation bronchoscopy (ENB) is a minimally invasive technology 
      enabling the navigation of bronchoscopic instruments to reach peripheral 
      pulmonary lesions (PPLs) that are otherwise challenging to reach. To date, ENB 
      has primarily been assessed in high-volume centres or within the framework of 
      multicentre trials. Consequently, the actual diagnostic yield in a real-world 
      setting, particularly among patients with varying levels of risk, remains largely 
      unknown.This prospective single-centre cohort study enrolled consecutive patients 
      with PPLs between February 2023 and April 2024. The Medtronic ILLUMISITE platform 
      was utilised to perform ENB procedures. The primary endpoints of the study were 
      the diagnostic yield of ENB as a standalone procedure, assessed at the end of the 
      intervention, and the overall diagnostic accuracy, which incorporated ENB in 
      combination with other diagnostic techniques, evaluated after a 12-month 
      follow-up period. The secondary endpoint was the rate of procedural 
      complications.A total of thirty patients participated in this study. The median 
      lesion size was 16.5 mm. ENB achieved a technical success rate of 93%. The 
      diagnostic yield was 57%. When considering the combination of ENB and other 
      diagnostic methods over the course of a year, the diagnostic accuracy increased 
      to 67%. Two cases of pneumothorax (6.6%), but no instances of bronchopulmonary 
      haemorrhage requiring intervention were observed.This small, single-centre, 
      real-world cohort study, which applied strict definitions for diagnostic yield 
      and accuracy, demonstrates that ENB-aided diagnosis can be successfully achieved 
      in approximately two-thirds of eligible patients, with a low rate of procedural 
      complications.
