DP  - 2026 Feb 28
TI  - Synergistic use of robotic navigational platform and ultrathin bronchoscopy in 
      diagnostic yield optimization for cavitary lung lesions: a case series.
PG  - 157
LID - 10.21037/jtd-2025-aw-2052 [doi]
LID - 157
AB  - BACKGROUND: Cavitary lung lesions represent a diagnostic challenge due to their 
      heterogeneous etiology, which includes infectious, inflammatory, and malignant 
      processes. Establishing an accurate diagnosis is essential, yet conventional 
      techniques such as computed tomography-guided transthoracic biopsy, surgical 
      biopsy and standard bronchoscopy may be limited by complication risk or 
      suboptimal yield. Recent advances in robotic and ultrathin bronchoscopy have 
      improved precision and reach, but their combined application remains 
      underexplored. CASE DESCRIPTION: We conducted a retrospective case series of six 
      consecutive patients with cavitary lung lesions evaluated at a tertiary academic 
      medical center. All procedures were performed by a single interventional 
      pulmonologist using a standardized workflow. Pre-procedural lesion marking and 
      airway mapping were completed in all cases with Intuitive PointPlan software to 
      generate a virtual navigation roadmap. Ultrathin flexible bronchoscopy (Vathin 
      disposable bronchoscope; 3.2-mm outer diameter, 1.2-mm working channel) was used 
      in all patients to access and sample the target cavities. In four cases performed 
      under general anesthesia, the IonTM robotic bronchoscopy platform was 
      additionally employed for navigational guidance and fluoroscopy-assisted biopsy, 
      followed by ultrathin bronchoscopy to allow direct intracavitary visualization 
      and targeted sampling. Successful navigation to the cavitary lesion was achieved 
      in all six procedures. The combined robotic-ultrathin bronchoscopy approach 
      provided additional diagnostic information beyond robotic-guided sampling alone 
      in two of four cases. No procedure-related pneumothorax or significant bleeding 
      occurred. One patient developed a transient post-procedural fever that resolved 
      with antibiotic therapy. At 3-month follow-up, all patients demonstrated 
      radiologic or symptomatic improvement consistent with the final diagnosis. 
      CONCLUSIONS: The combined use of navigational robotic and ultrathin flexible 
      bronchoscopy is a feasible, safe, and potentially superior approach for 
      evaluating cavitary lung lesions. This workflow integrates precise navigation 
      with direct intracavitary visualization, optimizing diagnostic yield while 
      minimizing procedural risk.
