DP  - 2026 Mar 6
TI  - Prevalence and Predictors of Eosinophilic Esophagitis (EoE) Among Children With 
      Severe Asthma.
LID - S2213-2198(26)00182-0 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jaip.2026.02.030 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Although previous studies have found an association between 
      eosinophilic esophagitis (EoE) and asthma, no studies to date have assessed the 
      prevalence or predictors of EoE among children with severe asthma. OBJECTIVE: To 
      assess the prevalence and predictors of EoE among children with severe asthma. 
      METHODS: This was a cross-sectional study of children with recurrent 
      wheeze/severe asthma who underwent bronchoscopy for respiratory symptoms and 
      esophagogastroduodenoscopy for gastrointestinal symptoms. EoE cases were defined 
      according to consensus guidelines. Diagnostic tests included bronchoalveolar 
      lavage for cell count/differential and measurements of blood absolute eosinophil 
      count (AEC) and total IgE. Differences in demographic and clinical 
      characteristics were compared using χ(2) tests and the Student t test; predictors 
      of EoE were assessed using logistic regression and receiver-operating 
      characteristic curves. RESULTS: A total of 214 children with problematic wheeze 
      or poorly controlled asthma and gastrointestinal symptoms were included. EoE 
      prevalence was 23.4%. EoE was associated with bronchoalveolar lavage eosinophilia 
      (odds ratio, 2.61; 95% CI, 1.13-6.00; P = .001), but this did not persist in 
      adjusted analyses. Patients with coexisting EoE had a higher prevalence of 
      allergic comorbidities and higher blood AEC (672.0 ± 1043.4/μL vs 235.4 ± 216.9; 
      P < .001). AEC demonstrated high diagnostic accuracy for EoE when used in 
      combination with the presence of allergic comorbidities. CONCLUSIONS: A high 
      prevalence of coexisting EoE was found in pediatric patients with severe asthma. 
      Those with severe asthma with EoE were more likely to have other allergic 
      conditions and higher blood AEC levels. Future studies should evaluate whether 
      AEC can be used to screen for EoE among children with severe asthma.
