TI  - An Ultrasensitive Electrochemical Pepsin Immunosensor Based on Heterogeneous 
      Ag@Co(OH)(2) NWs for Rapid Diagnostic Testing of Aspiration Pneumonia.
PG  - 13690-13699
LID - 10.1021/acsami.6c01477 [doi]
AB  - Aspiration pneumonia (AP), caused by the microinhalation of gastric contents, is 
      easy to miss because of its nonspecific symptoms and is associated with a high 
      mortality rate (46.1%) over a short disease course. Current diagnostic methods 
      for aspiration pneumonia, including medical imaging and bronchoscopy, are 
      difficult to use for onsite and rapid diagnosis because they require bulky and 
      time-consuming equipment. In this work, we propose an ultrasensitive 
      electrochemical immunosensor to achieve fast determination of the AP biomarker 
      (pepsin) in the bronchoalveolar lavage fluid (BALF) of AP patients. This 
      immunosensor works through the construction of heterogeneous Ag@Co(OH)(2) NWs to 
      simultaneously provide both high electrocatalysis and high conductivity. In terms 
      of architecture, Ag NWs can contribute abundant active sites for antibody 
      immobilization to achieve target capture ability while accelerating electron 
      transfer. In addition, the uniformly distributed Co(OH)(2) nanoparticles 
      intensify the response signal because of their high redox capacity. Through the 
      above synergetic effects, our immunosensor enables the recognition and reporting 
      of trace amounts of pepsin in patients' BALF within only 20 min, which is at 
      least two times faster than that of clinical techniques. Moreover, the high 
      diagnostic accuracy of the immunosensor is comparable to that of CT images from 
      clinical AP patients. This facile immunosensor is promising for providing new 
      insights into the early diagnosis of AP to guide clinical treatment onsite.
