VI  - 58
DP  - 2025
TI  - Pulmonary lymphangitic carcinomatosis mimicking interstitial lung disease.
PG  - 102334
LID - 10.1016/j.rmcr.2025.102334 [doi]
LID - 102334
AB  - BACKGROUND: Pulmonary Lymphangitic Carcinomatosis (PLC) is a rare and aggressive 
      form of metastatic spread. Pathophysiologically, PLC occurs through secondary 
      spread of tumor emboli to the pulmonary lymphatic system and further malignant 
      infiltration of pulmonary interstitium. Radiologic findings may mimic alternative 
      pulmonary pathology such as interstitial lung disease or infection leading to a 
      delayed diagnosis. We describe a case of PLC in a patient with metastatic breast 
      cancer and interesting, nonspecific computed tomography (CT) chest findings. CASE 
      PRESENTATION: A middle-aged female presented with progressive dyspnea and 
      non-productive cough. Thoracic CT showed diffuse patchy airspace consolidation 
      with smooth interlobular septal thickening. Echocardiography and right heart 
      catheterization revealed elevated right ventricular systolic pressure with normal 
      pulmonary capillary wedge pressure. Despite empiric treatment for pneumonia, her 
      hypoxia persisted. Bronchoscopy with biopsy identified a HER2-positive breast 
      carcinoma with metastatic lymphangitic spread, consistent with PLC. The patient 
      was initiated on palliative chemotherapy and eventually was referred to hospice 1 
      month after diagnosis. CONCLUSION: Atypical CT findings of PLC sometimes may 
      mimic interstitial lung disease. PLC is a rapidly progressive, aggressive disease 
      due to the nature of its staging at diagnosis. This case highlights the 
      importance of maintaining a high index of clinical suspicion and obtaining an 
      early tissue biopsy to establish definitive diagnosis.
