TI  - A case series of nonintubated robotic-assisted bronchoscopy: a feasible 
      alternative to intubated techniques.
PG  - 4051-4056
LID - 10.21037/tlcr-2025-169 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Robotic-assisted bronchoscopy (RAB) is widely used for pulmonary 
      lesion management but is limited by its requirement for endotracheal intubation. 
      In recent years, non-intubated anesthesia had emerged as an available alternative 
      for thoracic procedure. Integrating RAB with a nonintubated approach may overcome 
      current limitations, expand patient eligibility, and simplify procedural 
      logistics. This study aims to evaluate the safety and feasibility of 
      non-intubated RAB. CASE DESCRIPTION: There were seven patients with 10 lesions 
      underwent nonintubated RAB using the Monarch system. All procedures were 
      successfully performed, with an average lesion size of 1.4 cm and a mean 
      procedure time of 33.8 minutes. Pathology concordance was 100%, with 100% 
      navigation accuracy. No intraoperative or postoperative complications occurred. 
      Among them, five patients with positive diagnosis proceeded directly to 
      thoracoscopic surgery under a single anesthetic regimen, avoiding the need for 
      transitioning from single-lumen to double-lumen intubation. The mean interval 
      between biopsy and resection was only 24.2 minutes, and recovery was rapid, with 
      a mean postoperative recovery time of 26.7 minutes. CONCLUSIONS: Nonintubated RAB 
      offers a safe, efficient, and flexible alternative to traditional intubated 
      approach, particularly for patients with narrow airways or significant airway 
      obstruction. By eliminating the need for inhalational anesthesia, this approach 
      also aligns with sustainable practices.
