TI  - A Severe Case of Streptococcus pyogenes Pneumonia Following Influenza A Virus 
      Infection Diagnosed by FilmArray Pneumonia Panel and Bronchoalveolar Lavage.
PG  - e70267
LID - 10.1002/rcr2.70267 [doi]
LID - e70267
AB  - A previously healthy 63-year-old woman, unvaccinated for influenza A, had a mild 
      fever for a week followed by alerted consciousness. On the day of admission, she 
      developed shortness of breath, high fever, hypotension and alterations of 
      consciousness. Chest CT revealed rapidly progressing bilateral infiltrates. She 
      was diagnosed with disseminated intravascular coagulation, hypogammaglobulinemia 
      and acute renal failure, requiring intensive care for respiratory failure and 
      hypotension. A Sputum FilmArray Pneumonia Panel (PN panel) performed on admission 
      identified multiple pathogens, including influenza A virus and Streptococcus 
      pyogenes (S. pyogenes), although only S. pyogenes was cultured from 
      bronchoalveolar lavage fluid. Intensive care, including antibiotics and steroids, 
      led to clinical improvement. Blood cultures remained negative throughout the 
      course. Even healthy adults can develop severe S. pyogenes pneumonia following 
      influenza A infection. Rapid bacterial identification by PN panel and 
      bronchoscopy, along with an appropriate treatment strategy, may improve outcomes.
