OAB - This study analyzed the clinical characteristics, treatment, and outcomes of 233 
      hospitalized children aged 0–1 years with pertussis, revealing significant 
      differences between those requiring respiratory support and those who did not.The 
      findings indicated that children in the respiratory support group had a lower 
      vaccination rate and experienced more severe symptoms, including cyanosis, 
      respiratory distress, and higher incidences of RSV complications.Age emerged as 
      an independent protective factor, with younger children being more likely to 
      require respiratory support and experiencing more severe clinical manifestations 
      of pertussis.
