TI  - Acquired tracheoesophageal fistula in an adult due to mango endocarp: a case 
      report.
PG  - 2440-2443
LID - 10.1097/MS9.0000000000003111 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION AND IMPORTANCE: Tracheoesophageal fistulas (TEF) resulting from 
      foreign body (FB) ingestion often present late, leading to delayed diagnosis. To 
      the best of our knowledge, this is the first reported case of TEF caused by the 
      accidental ingestion of a mango endocarp in the English literature. CASE 
      PRESENTATION: A 27-year-old female was referred for persistent cough for 4 
      months, following accidental ingestion of a mango endocarp. She initially 
      complained of short-lived throat pain, foreign body sensation, and difficulty 
      swallowing. At our facility, imaging revealed a TEF at the T1 vertebral level. 
      Intraoperative localization was achieved with a bronchoscope and the fistulous 
      tract was dissected and excised. The esophagus and trachea were repaired in two 
      and one layers, respectively, and a sternohyoid flap was mobilized to buttress 
      the repairs. She had a smooth recovery, with normal swallowing and voice 
      preservation, and remains asymptomatic at 2 years. CLINICAL DISCUSSION: Although 
      the majority of esophageal FBs are uncomplicated, delayed presentations can lead 
      to severe complications like bleeding, perforation, or TEF. In our case, the 
      initial symptoms of throat pain and mild dysphagia might have been the result of 
      an initial laceration by the mango endocarp or impaction resulting in ulceration. 
      The ulcer might have eroded deep into the trachea causing a fistula due to delay 
      in seeking care. CONCLUSION: Mango endocarp ingestion can be a rare cause of TEF 
      in adults and has to be considered in an appropriate setting.
