IS  - 1099-0496 (Electronic)
IS  - 1099-0496 (Linking)
VI  - 60 Suppl 1
DP  - 2025 Mar
TI  - The lung in inborn errors of immunity.
PG  - S29-S31
LID - 10.1002/ppul.27258 [doi]
AB  - The lungs are integral to immune defense, and inborn errors of immunity (IEI) 
      often manifest as lung disease. Lung complications of IEI can involve the 
      airways, alveolar spaces, interstitium, vasculature, and pleura. Accurate 
      identification of these lung disease patterns requires a thorough clinical 
      history, physical examination, and high-resolution computed tomography (HRCT), as 
      lung imaging patterns guide further respiratory and immunological evaluations. 
      Respiratory assessment may also include pulmonary function tests, bronchoscopy 
      with bronchoalveolar lavage, and, in some cases, lung biopsy. Additionally, 
      molecular diagnosis of underlying immune defects, typically through comprehensive 
      clinical phenotyping, functional immune studies, and genetic testing, is crucial 
      for informing patient management and guiding targeted therapies. Importantly, 
      given the complexity of IEI, a multidisciplinary approach is necessary. 
      Furthermore, ongoing research is required to refine therapies and improve 
      outcomes for lung complications.
