LID - e272527 [pii]
LID - 10.1136/bcr-2026-272527 [doi]
AB  - A man in his late 60s presented with rapidly progressive dyspnoea and inspiratory 
      stridor requiring emergency intubation. Bronchoscopy demonstrated critical 
      bilateral main bronchial compression, and biopsy confirmed small cell lung cancer 
      (SCLC) with bulky mediastinal disease. Deliberately dose-reduced carboplatin and 
      etoposide were initiated. Despite prophylactic hydration and allopurinol, he 
      developed fulminant tumour lysis syndrome (TLS) approximately 72 hours after 
      chemotherapy initiation, meeting Cairo-Bishop criteria and died despite 
      rasburicase and maximal supportive care.TLS is rare in solid tumours, and in 
      SCLC, it is typically reported following spontaneous lysis or full-dose 
      chemotherapy. This case demonstrates that even dose-reduced chemotherapy, when 
      used emergently in bulky, chemosensitive disease, can precipitate catastrophic 
      TLS. It highlights critical airway compromise in SCLC as a high-risk phenotype 
      and highlights the need for heightened vigilance and anticipatory management when 
      balancing urgent airway stabilisation against systemic therapy.
