TI  - A case report of tuberculous fibrosing mediastinitis: focusing on the diagnostic 
      value of three-dimensional reconstruction from pulmonary contrast-enhanced CT and 
      bronchoscopic features.
PG  - 58
LID - 10.21037/acr-2025-301 [doi]
LID - 58
AB  - BACKGROUND: Fibrosing mediastinitis (FM) is a rare disease characterized by 
      excessive fibrotic tissue proliferation in the mediastinum, often leading to 
      compression of vital structures. Tuberculosis is a common cause in certain 
      regions. Diagnosis is challenging due to non-specific symptoms. This report 
      explores the integrated diagnostic value of advanced three-dimensional (3D) 
      computed tomography (CT) reconstruction and bronchoscopy in tuberculous fibrosing 
      mediastinitis (TFM). CASE DESCRIPTION: A 69-year-old female presented with a 
      2-year history of progressive exertional dyspnea. Chest contrast-enhanced CT 
      revealed mediastinal soft tissue infiltration, calcified lymph nodes, and right 
      middle lobe opacities. 3D reconstruction from the CT data precisely demonstrated 
      occlusion of the right middle lobe medial segmental bronchus, severe stenosis of 
      the lateral segmental and right lower lobe dorsal segmental bronchi, and an 
      extrinsic "knife-cut" stenosis of the right main pulmonary artery. Bronchoscopy 
      confirmed multiple distorted and occluded bronchi with anthracotic pigmentation. 
      Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex nucleic acid was detected in bronchoalveolar 
      lavage fluid. The patient was diagnosed with TFM and showed symptomatic 
      improvement after initiating anti-tuberculosis therapy combined with 
      corticosteroids. CONCLUSIONS: The integration of pulmonary contrast-enhanced CT 
      3D reconstruction with bronchoscopic evaluation provides a powerful, minimally 
      invasive approach for diagnosing TFM. This multimodal strategy enhances the 
      visualization of complex mediastinal involvement, aids in differential diagnosis, 
      and can critically inform management decisions to avoid unnecessary high-risk 
      procedures.
