TI  - Antisynthetase Syndrome Mimicking Pulmonary Infection: A Diagnostic Lesson From 
      County-Managed Hospital.
PG  - e72085
LID - 10.1002/ccr3.72085 [doi]
LID - e72085
AB  - Antisynthetase syndrome (ASS) is a rare chronic autoimmune disease classified as 
      a distinct subtype of idiopathic inflammatory myopathy, characterized by the 
      presence of anti-aminoacyl-transfer RNA synthetase (anti-ARS) antibodies. Typical 
      clinical features include myositis, interstitial lung disease (ILD), arthritis, 
      fever, mechanic's hands, and Raynaud's phenomenon, with a predominance in 
      middle-aged to elderly females. We report an atypical case of a 75-year-old male 
      presenting with a 6-month history of anorexia and fatigue, followed by a 3-week 
      progression of chest tightness and dyspnea. Initially misdiagnosed as a pulmonary 
      infection, the patient showed no improvement after two weeks of antimicrobial 
      therapy. Diagnosis was ultimately confirmed through bronchoscopy and detection of 
      anti-Jo-1 antibodies. Subsequent corticosteroid treatment led to complete 
      radiographic resolution and significant clinical improvement. This case 
      underscores the importance of considering ASS in elderly male patients to prevent 
      misdiagnosis and ensure timely intervention.
