TI  - Association between exposure to microplastics and lipid disorders: A case-control 
      study.
LID - S2090-1232(26)00152-9 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jare.2026.02.020 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Evidence on the potential effect of internal exposures to 
      microplastics on lipid disorders is scarce. OBJECTIVES: We aimed to explore the 
      association between microplastics and lipid disorders. METHODS: We conducted a 
      case-control study from 2020 to 2022 in Zhuhai, China, involving patients aged 
      ≥18 years who underwent fiberoptic bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage fluid 
      (BALF) collected. Microplastics in BALF were identified and quantified using 
      laser direct infrared spectroscopy. Associations between microplastic exposure 
      and lipid disorders were analyzed using logistic regression, linear regression, 
      and restricted cubic splines. RESULTS: A total of 239 patients were included in 
      the study. Fourteen main types of microplastics were detected in the BALF, with 
      polyethylene (PE) being dominant. Each 100-unit increase in overall 
      microplastics, total PE, and PE was significantly associated with odds ratios for 
      hyperlipidemia of 1.054 (1.006, 1.113), 1.076 (1.001, 1.160), and 1.081 (1.002, 
      1.169), respectively; and for dyslipidemia of 1.061 (1.012, 1.121), 1.085 (1.009, 
      1.170), and 1.087 (1.008, 1.176), respectively. Non-linear relationships were not 
      observed for microplastics on hyperlipidemia and dyslipidemia. In addition, 
      exposure to overall microplastics, total PE, and PE was associated with elevated 
      total cholesterol levels. CONCLUSIONS: Internal exposure to microplastics is 
      significantly associated with lipid disorders, providing novel evidence of 
      adverse impacts of microplastics on lipid metabolism.
