TI  - Pulmonary Involvement Successfully Treated With Infliximab in a Patient With 
      Pyoderma Gangrenosum and Rheumatoid Arthritis: A Case Report.
PG  - e70485
LID - 10.1002/rcr2.70485 [doi]
LID - e70485
AB  - Pulmonary involvement of pyoderma gangrenosum (PG) is rare and has been observed 
      in few patients with rheumatoid arthritis (RA). We describe the case of a 
      72-year-old woman with RA who simultaneously developed multiple cavitary 
      pulmonary nodules and worsening cutaneous PG. Differential diagnoses included 
      PG-related pulmonary lesions and rheumatoid nodules, which may cavitate and are 
      difficult to distinguish radiologically and histologically. Bronchoscopy and 
      video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery were performed. A histopathological 
      evaluation revealed nonspecific findings, and infection was considered unlikely. 
      Infliximab (IFX) was initiated because limited reports have suggested its 
      potential benefit for pulmonary PG and potential usefulness for lesions that 
      represent rheumatoid nodules. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first 
      report of pulmonary involvement in a patient with PG and RA that was successfully 
      treated with IFX. This case highlights diagnostic challenges and suggests that 
      tumour necrosis factor-α inhibitors may be a promising therapeutic option for 
      select cases.
