TI  - Cerebral arterial air embolism complicating robotic guided lung biopsy: A call 
      for vigilance.
PG  - 102320
LID - 10.1016/j.rmcr.2025.102320 [doi]
LID - 102320
AB  - Flexible bronchoscopy has been extensively studied and is associated with a 
      relatively low incidence of mortality and morbidity. Robotic bronchoscopy, a more 
      recent advancement, has demonstrated non-inferiority to transthoracic needle lung 
      biopsy and maintains an excellent safety profile. Vascular air embolism, while 
      exceedingly rare, has been infrequently reported as a complication of therapeutic 
      bronchoscopy. Despite its rarity, this complication carries a considerable risk 
      of morbidity and mortality. Although several patient- and procedure-related risk 
      factors have been identified, the infrequency of these cases presents challenges 
      in elucidating the mechanisms involved and establishing optimal diagnostic 
      strategies. Herein, we report what we believe to be the first documented case of 
      cerebral arterial gas embolism following robotic-guided lung biopsy.
