TI  - [Advancements in Research on Preoperative Localization of Pulmonary Nodules].
PG  - 385-390
LID - 10.3779/j.issn.1009-3419.2025.101.07 [doi]
AB  - In recent years, the widespread application of chest computed tomography (CT) 
      screening has led to a significant increase in the detection rate of pulmonary 
      nodules. As a critical diagnostic tool for early-stage lung cancer, 
      video-assisted thoracic surgery (VATS) has emerged as the preferred therapeutic 
      approach for pulmonary nodules. Clinical evidence demonstrates that precise 
      preoperative localization significantly enhances surgical success rates (reducing 
      conversion to thoracotomy), minimizes complications, and shortens operation time. 
      This comprehensive review systematically evaluates six cutting-edge localization 
      techniques: percutaneous puncture-assisted localization, electromagnetic 
      navigation bronchoscopy (ENB) localization, 3D-printed auxiliary localization, 
      basin-analysis-based localization, robotic navigation system localization, and 
      mixed reality (MR)-guided localization. By critically analyzing their operational 
      principles, efficacy, safety profiles, and clinical applicability, this paper 
      aims to provide evidence-based recommendations for optimizing clinical 
      decision-making in pulmonary nodule management. .
