AB  - A 4-year-old male castrated domestic shorthair cat was diagnosed with a urethral 
      obstruction after presenting for vomiting, inappetence, and inappropriate 
      urination. The cat developed iatrogenic hypercalcemia (ionized calcium 
      > 4 mmol/L, total calcium 57 mg/dL) and an acute kidney injury (creatinine 
      9.81 mg/dL) suspected to be a consequence of an overdose of calcium gluconate. 
      Approximately 12 h after admission, the cat became comatose and developed 
      cardiovascular instability. A 6-h hemodialysis treatment was performed using 
      continuous venovenous hemodiafiltration on the Prismaflex platform and 
      hypercalcemia resolved during treatment. The cat's creatinine normalized 
      (1.01 mg/dL) within 72 h and the cat was discharged after 6 days of 
      hospitalization. The cat subsequently developed signs of upper respiratory and 
      gastrointestinal disease. Diagnostics including upper gastrointestinal endoscopy 
      and bronchoscopy showed evidence of diffuse calcification of the trachea and 
      gastric mucosa. This case demonstrates the successful application of hemodialysis 
      in veterinary medicine to treat an acute kidney injury and iatrogenic 
      hypercalcemia and provides unique documentation of its potential long-term 
      sequelae.
