TI  - Approach to prevention and management of chronic lung allograft dysfunction in 
      North American lung transplant centers.
LID - S1053-2498(26)01948-0 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.healun.2026.06.004 [doi]
AB  - Chronic lung allograft dysfunction (CLAD) is the major barrier to long-term 
      survival after lung transplantation, but literature documenting contemporary 
      practice patterns and best practices for management are lacking. We sought to 
      characterize monitoring and lung function-dependent management strategies across 
      adult North American lung transplant centers. A RedCap survey was distributed to 
      21 centers accounting for nearly half of North American lung transplants. Routine 
      immunosuppression and post-transplant graft monitoring protocols were similar 
      among centers. Prophylactic azithromycin was routinely used by 7 (33.3%) centers, 
      and respondents had substantial uncertainty regarding its effectiveness. A 
      persistent ≥10% FEV(₁) decline was considered clinically meaningful by most 
      centers and triggered similar evaluations, including bronchoscopy, DSA testing, 
      and chest CT. However, both the lung function thresholds prompting intervention 
      and specific therapeutic strategies varied substantially. These findings 
      highlight significant heterogeneity in CLAD management and support the need for 
      prospective studies to inform evidence-based approaches.
