PMC - PMC13155028
OAB - Bronchoscopic cryoimmunotherapy in lung cancer: utilizing targeted freezing to 
      awaken the immune systemLung cancer is the leading cause of cancer death in the 
      United States, even with recent advances in cancer therapies. Many patients 
      cannot undergo radiation or surgery, and new therapies that are tolerable, while 
      also boosting the immune system, are needed. Cryoablation is a technique that 
      destroys tumors by freezing them, which is believed to have the unique effect of 
      preserving tumor proteins and surrounding tissue structure, which may help the 
      immune system recognize and attack tumor cells.Bronchoscopic cryoimmunotherapy 
      (BCI) is a special form of cryoablation delivered through a bronchoscope passed 
      into the lungs. Instead of trying to destroy a tumor, BCI freezes only a portion 
      of the tumor, which has been proposed to cause the release of molecules that can 
      activate cancer-fighting T cells, reduce suppressive regulatory T cells, and 
      activate immunostimulating pathways, potentially leading to local, and even 
      distant, antitumor effects.Early preclinical and clinical studies show that BCI 
      may safely cause immune activation and may enhance the effects of other powerful 
      cancer therapies, such as anti–PD-1 therapy. However, current evidence is based 
      on small, early or retrospective studies, often with mixed patient populations 
      and without standard protocols. While the biological rationale is strong, proof 
      that BCI leads to consistent, meaningful improvements in survival and quality of 
      life is still lacking. Larger, well-designed trials are needed to define which 
      patients benefit most, how best to combine BCI with other therapies, and how to 
      optimize treatment timing and technique.
