IS  - 0975-5764 (Linking)
DP  - 2026 Apr 7
TI  - Endobronchial Lipoma in a Young Patient: A Rare and Atypical Case Report.
LID - 10.4103/aam.aam_62_26 [doi]
AB  - Endobronchial lipoma (EL) is a rare, benign pulmonary tumor that frequently 
      causes diagnostic delays due to its nonspecific presentation. We report a case of 
      a 32-year-old male chronic smoker who presented with a 15-day history of dry 
      cough and tachypnea. Chest X-ray showed left lower lobe atelectasis. 
      Contrast-enhanced computed tomography revealed a fat-density lesion in the carina 
      on the left side extending into the left main bronchus, causing distal 
      obstruction and left lower lobar collapse. Diagnostic bronchoscopy confirmed a 
      large, pedunculated mass completely occluding the left main bronchus. The mass 
      was successfully resected through electrocautery snare and cryoextraction. 
      Histopathological examination confirmed a well-encapsulated lesion composed of 
      mature adipocytes, consistent with EL. This case highlights the importance of 
      considering EL in patients with obstructive symptoms and demonstrates the 
      efficacy of bronchoscopic resection as the first-line therapeutic intervention.
