TI  - Pulmonary mucormycosis and Achromobacter xylosoxidans co-infection in an 
      immunocompromised host: a case report.
PG  - 8860-8865
LID - 10.1097/MS9.0000000000004024 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION AND IMPORTANCE: Pulmonary mucormycosis (PM) is a severe fungal 
      infection threatening immunocompromised individuals, especially with uncontrolled 
      diabetes. Co-infection with bacteria like Achromobacter xylosoxidans complicates 
      management. We report a rare and fatal case of PM co-infected with A. 
      xylosoxidans, which was further complicated by a subsequent SARS-CoV-2 
      superinfection. CASE PRESENTATION: A 55-year-old female with poorly controlled 
      type 2 diabetes mellitus and multiple comorbidities presented with worsening 
      dyspnea. Investigations revealed severe hyperglycemia (927 mg/dL), hyponatremia, 
      and imaging showed a large right upper lobe cavitary lesion. Bronchoscopy 
      confirmed acute necrotizing pneumonia with Zygomycetes (Mucor) elements and grew 
      >100 000 CFU/mL A. xylosoxidans/denitrificans. Computed tomography also revealed 
      extensive nasopharyngeal mucormycosis. Her hospital course was complicated by a 
      hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state, pulseless electrical activity cardiac arrest 
      secondary to hemoptysis, and a subsequent SARS-CoV-2 superinfection, ultimately 
      resulting in death. CLINICAL DISCUSSION: This case exemplifies the diagnostic 
      difficulties of PM, often mimicking other pulmonary conditions and requiring 
      invasive testing for confirmation. Uncontrolled diabetes was a critical 
      predisposing factor. The presence of A. xylosoxidans, an intrinsically resistant 
      bacterium, alongside PM created significant therapeutic hurdles. The subsequent 
      SARS-CoV-2 superinfection likely further compromised the patient's critical 
      status. The subsequent SARS-CoV-2 infection likely further compromised the 
      patient's status. The case underscores the lethal synergy of these sequential 
      opportunistic infections, and the poor prognosis associated with PM, especially 
      when compounded by critical illness and co-infections. CONCLUSION: High suspicion 
      for invasive fungal infections is crucial in high-risk patients with refractory 
      pneumonia. Prompt invasive diagnostics and awareness of bacterial co-pathogens 
      are essential, as outcomes remain poor despite aggressive therapy.
