TI  - Acid and weakly acidic reflux characteristics in pediatric airway malacia by pH 
      and multiple intraluminal impedance monitoring: a retrospective cohort study.
PG  - 576
LID - 10.1007/s00431-025-06330-2 [doi]
AB  - The objective of this study was to analyze the 24-h pH and multiple intraluminal 
      impedance (pH-MII) monitoring results in pediatric patients with airway malacia, 
      with the aim of comprehensively understanding the characteristics of 
      gastroesophageal reflux (GER) in these patients and exploring their potential 
      roles in the pathogenesis of airway malacia. This retrospective cohort study 
      enrolled pediatric patients who underwent 24-h pH-MII monitoring and bronchoscopy 
      at Maternal and Child Health Hospital of Hubei Province from January 2017 to 
      October 2023. Among 64 pediatric patients with airway malacia, 24 (37.5%) 
      patients met the diagnostic criteria for GERD. Compared to those without airway 
      malacia, patients with airway malacia showed significantly higher acid reflux 
      index (RI) (3.00% vs 1.60%, p = 0.021), acidic liquid reflux (8.50% vs 5.00%, 
      p = 0.016), and pH reflux episodes (51.00 vs 26.00, p = 0.047). Age-stratified 
      analysis showed that older children (≥1 year) exhibited higher acid reflux (36.0% 
      vs 26.0%, p = 0.047), while infants (<1 year) exhibited elevated weakly acidic 
      reflux (64.0% vs 53.0%, p = 0.013). Analysis of the receiver operating 
      characteristic (ROC) curve for diagnosis identified sex, age, RI, and acid reflux 
      as factors associated with airway malacia. CONCLUSION: PH-MII characterized 
      age-dependent reflux profiles in airway malacia pediatrics. Acid reflux appears 
      to play a pivotal pathogenic role in older children, whereas weakly acidic reflux 
      is predominant in infants. These findings support age-tailored strategies: acid 
      suppression for older children with high acid reflux and dietary modifications to 
      reduce weak acidic exposure in infants. WHAT IS KNOWN: • A large number of 
      studies have confirmed that gastroesophageal reflux disease is one of the 
      etiological factors of airway malacia in children. WHAT IS NEW: • The reflux 
      characteristics of children with airway malacia in different age groups are 
      different, and the methods of anti-reflux treatment should also be different.
