TI  - Clinical characteristics and predictive models of plastic bronchitis caused by 
      mycoplasma pneumoniae pneumonia in children.
PG  - 37-43
LID - S0929-6646(25)00379-1 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jfma.2025.07.012 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: The prevalence of mycoplasma pneumoniae pneumonia (MPP) in China in 
      2023 is accompanied by an increase in cases of plastic bronchitis (PB), which 
      provides an opportunity to explore its risk factors. METHODS: A retrospective 
      analysis of 282 children with MPP who underwent fiber-optic bronchoscopy (FOB) at 
      our institution was performed. Based on the results of FOB, the participants were 
      divided into two groups: PB and non-PB. The basic information, clinical 
      manifestations, treatments, laboratory results, and chest imaging findings of the 
      two groups were compared. For model construction, 200 children were selected for 
      the training set, while 82 children were selected for external validation in the 
      validation set. Least absolute shrinkage selection operator regression and 
      various cross-validations were used to select potential variables. Multivariate 
      logistic regression analysis is used to structure nomograms. Use ROC curves, 
      Akaike information criterion, and calibration curves to screen and evaluate the 
      model. RESULTS: Multivariable logistic regression analysis identified risk 
      factors for PB caused by MPP, including age, peak body temperature, atelectasis, 
      low serum albumin, and lactic dehydrogenase. We have developed a nomogram based 
      on this. The area under the curve of the nomogram was 0.8768 (95 % CI: 
      0.8206-0.933) in the training set and 0.8399 (95 % CI: 0.7428-0.9371) in the 
      validation set, demonstrating good discriminative ability and accuracy. 
      CONCLUSION: We developed and validated a nomogram to predict PB caused by MPP in 
      this study. Using the nomogram can assist in clinically assessing the formation 
      of PB and selecting appropriate treatment strategies.
