TI  - [Complete left lung atelectasis as the initial presentation of allergic 
      bronchopulmonary aspergillosis: a case report].
PG  - 661-665
LID - 10.3760/cma.j.cn112147-20250113-00032 [doi]
AB  - This paper reports a case of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA) 
      presenting with acute respiratory failure and complete left lung atelectasis on 
      imaging. The patient had no prior history of respiratory disease and experienced 
      a sudden onset of symptoms, with partial atelectasis of the left lung rapidly 
      progressing to complete collapse. Significant clinical improvement was observed 
      after bronchoscopic removal of a suspected "foreign body" from the left main 
      bronchus, leading to an initial misdiagnosis of bronchial foreign body. However, 
      one month later, follow-up imaging revealed a new shadow in the left upper lobe. 
      Further investigation confirmed the diagnosis of ABPA caused by sensitization to 
      Aspergillus terreus.The patient was treated with two courses of systemic 
      corticosteroids combined with antifungal therapy, resulting in effective disease 
      control. Over a six-year follow-up period, the patient maintained a favorable 
      prognosis with no progression to late-stage pulmonary complications. Complete 
      lung atelectasis-type ABPA poses significant diagnostic challenges in its early 
      stages and remains rarely reported in the domestic literature, leading to a high 
      risk of misdiagnosis or delayed diagnosis. This case report aims to raise 
      clinical awareness and facilitate earlier recognition of this uncommon 
      presentation among healthcare professionals.
