IS  - 1757-790X (Electronic)
IS  - 1757-790X (Linking)
VI  - 18
IP  - 1
DP  - 2025 Jan 8
TI  - Nasal myiasis: an infrequent cause of sinusitis in a critical care patient.
LID - e263124 [pii]
LID - 10.1136/bcr-2024-263124 [doi]
AB  - A patient in his 70s, admitted to the cardiac intensive care unit with 
      cardiogenic shock, unexpectedly presented with nasal myiasis during a workup for 
      persistently elevated inflammatory markers. CT scans revealed sinusitis and 
      bronchial secretions, while bronchoscopy identified mucus with positive pathogen 
      testing. Nasal endoscopy was crucial in diagnosing myiasis, and immediate 
      mechanical removal of larvae was performed. Definitive treatment involved 
      surgical debridement, turbinectomy and sinus cleansing, leading to a successful 
      recovery without complications. This case emphasises the importance of 
      considering nasal myiasis as a rare but potential cause of sinusitis in 
      critically ill patients, even in non-endemic regions. Early diagnosis using 
      appropriate imaging and endoscopic techniques, followed by prompt treatment, is 
      essential for preventing severe complications. Increased awareness among 
      healthcare professionals is vital for the timely recognition and management of 
      this condition in critical care settings.
