DP  - 2025 Mar 15
TI  - Pulmonary Mycobacterium heckeshornense Infection in which the Causative 
      Microorganism Was Difficult to Identify.
PG  - 911-915
LID - 10.2169/internalmedicine.3837-24 [doi]
AB  - A 44-year-old woman underwent a follow-up examination for Crohn's disease 9 years 
      ago. Chest computed tomography (CT) showed an infiltration shadow with a cavity 
      in the right upper lobe. After a CT-guided lung biopsy, epitheloid granuloma was 
      noted, and an acid-fast bacilli examination was smear-positive, but a culture 
      examination was negative. Because the abnormal chest shadow with cavity gradually 
      increased and right shoulder pain appeared, we performed bronchoscopy again six 
      months later. Mycobacterium heckeshornense was isolated from the bronchoalveolar 
      lavage fluid specimen, so we diagnosed her with pulmonary M. heckeshornense 
      disease. Isoniazid, rifampicin, and ethambutol were administered, and the 
      abnormal chest shadow improved.
