TI  - A Case of Asymptomatic Tracheoesophageal Fistula.
PG  - 403S-406S
LID - 10.1177/01455613231200810 [doi]
AB  - The tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF) is an abnormal flow between the esophagus and 
      the trachea. Most patients with TEF experience severe symptoms. Asymptomatic TEF 
      is rare. In this case report, a 47-year-old woman planned to undergo orthopedic 
      surgery under general anesthesia. She had no symptoms related to TEF, and the 
      preoperative chest computed tomography was also normal. However, there was 
      significant airway resistance after induction. Using a fiber bronchoscope, a TEF 
      was discovered. The TEF found after anesthesia due to high airway pressure is 
      unusual, and the outcome and treatment of these patients need to be further 
      discussed.
