TI  - Upper and Lower Respiratory Mucous Membrane Plasmacytosis with a Cobblestone 
      Appearance: a Case Report.
PG  - 271S-274S
LID - 10.1177/01455613221134412 [doi]
AB  - A 53-year-old woman with a recurrent sore throat, xerostomia, nasal obstruction, 
      and nasal discharge came to our hospital. At the first visit, she did not have a 
      fever, but a blood test showed an intense inflammatory response. She had oral 
      mucosal erosion. She developed dyspnea 2 months later. Nasendoscopy, 
      laryngoscopy, and bronchoscopy showed upper and lower respiratory mucosa 
      cobblestone appearance. Microscopy of the nasal and pharyngeal mucosa biopsy 
      found regular mononuclear inflammatory cell dense submucosal infiltrate, mainly 
      plasma cells. A mucous membrane plasmacytosis diagnosis was made. Prednisolone 
      1 mg/kg/day rapidly improved bronchial symptoms and cobblestone appearance.
